\cf0 The policy estimates the ball\'92s horizontal intercept at the paddle row and compares it with the paddle position. The first hidden unit computes a signed intercept error directly from the ball and trail channels. For a descending ball at 
\f1\fs26 (r, c)
\f0\fs24 , the trail identifies horizontal direction: the projected intercept is 
\f1\fs26 c + 9 - r
\f0\fs24  when moving right and 
\f1\fs26 c + r - 9
\f0\fs24  when moving left. Row- and column-dependent weights encode these expressions without explicitly extracting coordinates.\
The second hidden unit estimates vertical direction. Four tanh units in the second layer form a nonlinear interaction between intercept error and vertical direction, suppressing movement while the ball is rising and activating it while descending. The remaining second-layer unit passes through the intercept error. Output weights favor left or right when the paddle is more than approximately one cell from the predicted intercept; a no-op bias favors holding position when it is close. Up, down, and fire are suppressed.\
The projection is exact for ordinary descending diagonal motion. Brick and wall collisions can make it temporarily inaccurate, but the policy recomputes from the next observation. This is a hand-designed policy, not an empirically evaluated one.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

